<worksheet>
<title>Completing the Square</title>
<objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this section we will see how to:
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Complete the square of a quadratic expression.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>
<p>
    Completing the square is an algebraic technique for converting a quadratic equation in standard form to another form called vertex form. We will learn more about vertex 
    form in a few lessons, when we discuss graphing quadratic equations. For now, we will see steps used to complete the square.
</p>
<example>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Complete the square of the quadratic <m>x^2-14x+34</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>(x-7)^2-15</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
          We want to create a perfect square trinomial. To do this, divide the number in the middle term by two and then square it. <m>-14/2 = -7</m> and <m>(-7)^2 = 49</m>.
          Now add and subtract this to our expression. <m>x^2-14x+49-49+34</m>. The first three terms now form a perfect square trinomial. <m>x^2-14x+49 = (x-7)^2</m>. 
          Add <m>-49+34 = -15</m>. Our original expression is now <m>(x-7)^2-15</m>
        </p>
        </solution>
</example>
<example>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Complete the square of the quadratic <m>2x^2+20x+96</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>2(x+5)^2+46</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
          First factor out the GCF 2 to get <m>2(x^2+10x+48)</m>. We now want to create a perfect square trinomial for what is inside the parentheses. To do this, divide
          the number in the middle term by two and then square it. <m>10/2 = 5</m> and <m>(5)^2 = 25</m>. Now add and subtract this to our expression. 
          <m>2(x^2+10x+25-25+48)</m>. The first three terms now inside the parentheses form a perfect square trinomial. <m>x^2+10x+25 = (x+5)^2</m>. 
          Add <m>-25+48 = 23</m>. Our original expression is now <m>2((x+5)^2+23) = 2(x+5)^2+46</m>
        </p>
        </solution>
</example>
<p>
    <alert>Try it Now!</alert>
</p>
<exercise>
    <introduction>
    <p>
        Complete the square of the following quadratic expressions.
    </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>x^2+8x+12</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>x^2+4x-45</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>x^2+11x+24</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>2x^2+8x+14</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>
</worksheet>